window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"AI and Occupational Health: understand, assess, prevent","description":"Understand how artificial intelligence affects work, assess its uses and prevent risks to health, organisation and skills.","keywords":"Resource · Training · Prevention Artificial intelligence and occupational health. Artificial intelligence is transforming work, its organisation and the practices of professionals who support employers and workers. AI & Occupational Health brings together the knowledge needed to understand these changes, prevent their effects on health and use AI with discernment in professional practice. An editorial initiative by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health · Updated 30 August 2026 Start the pathway ↓ Explore the website Open pathway No technical prerequisite Sourced content Understand what transforms work Thomas Cole The Consummation of Empire · 1836 Technologies transform societies through their uses as much as through their capabilities. Understanding AI at work therefore means examining both what the tool makes possible and what it transforms in the organisation. Essential An information, training and prevention resource. In occupational health, AI is both a professional tool and an organisational transformation. Its effects on workload, autonomy, skills, teams and health depend on actual use, objectives and deployment conditions — not only on the performance of the model. AI & Occupational Health offers an open pathway to understand systems, identify risks, assess uses, organise prevention and govern deployments. Three verifiable assets Go directly to evidence and tools. The site does not rest on summary pages alone: it connects an institutional publication, a reference page on psychosocial risks and a transparent exploratory tool. PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS · REFERENCE PAGE Six factors, three mechanisms and prevention measures. A synthesis of the possible psychosocial transformations, grounded in real work and primary sources. Read the psychosocial analysis → INRS · TF 335 A published study of 385 occupational-physician recommendations. The occupational-health services page sets out the method, the results, the limits and the conditions for use as a second reader. Examine the study and its limits → T0 · EXPLORATORY TOOL Assess before starting a pilot. Eighteen questions to open up discussion of the task, the review burden, autonomy, skills and stopping criteria. Open the T0 questionnaire → How are sources, evidence levels and corrections managed? Read the editorial method → 01 / Two inseparable dimensions Understanding AI at work requires a double reading. A transformation of work to assess and prevent, and a professional tool whose uses may be valuable when properly governed. 01 · Transformation o","url":"en/"},{"title":"Dr Charles Broutin — AI, Occupational Health and Real Work","description":"Background, publications and activities of Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health.","keywords":"About · Occupational medicine · Artificial intelligence Charles Broutin, occupational physician working at the intersection of AI and real work. As an occupational physician, AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health, author and trainer, I develop resources to examine professional AI uses and their effects on health. Explore my background ↓ View publications Clinical practice Collective prevention Independent resource Professional profile Paris · France Dr Charles Broutin Occupational physician · AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health Role SPSTN · Training & Methods Unit Contact LinkedIn profile ↗ Physician Occupational health Practice grounded in contact with workers, occupations and organisations. SFST Artificial intelligence lead Monitoring and knowledge-sharing for occupational health professionals. SPSTN Training & methods Head of the unit devoted to tools, practices and training. Independent Editorial project Dated, sourced content that is corrected as evidence develops. 01 / Background TRAINING Occupational medicine · University of Lille Clinical and preventive training focused on the links between health, activity and organisation. 2023 University diploma in artificial intelligence in health Additional training to understand models, their uses and limits in health. 2025 Medical residency thesis · Bossons Futé Prize Research on using large language models to identify wording defects in occupational physician recommendations. TODAY Physician, trainer, author and AI lead Research, monitoring and knowledge-sharing devoted to AI uses at work. 02 / Publications Research connecting language models, work quality and prevention. Two publications addressing different questions: the psychosocial effects of introducing LLMs and their use as a second reader of medical wording. 2025 Psychosocial risks Large language models and emerging psychosocial issues at work. The article proposes examining the introduction of LLMs through the six factors in the Gollac report: intensity, emotional demands, autonomy, social relationships, value conflicts and insecurity. Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement , 86, 102877. Open the DOI ↗ 2026 Quality of recommendations Artificial intelligence to improve the precision of occupational physician recommendations. The study evaluates a language model as a second reader for identifying several categories of defects, without replacing work-post analysis or the occupational physician’s decision. Références en Santé au Travail , TF 335, no. 186, June 2026. Read the article on the INRS ","url":"en/about/"},{"title":"CSE and artificial intelligence: role and consultation","description":"What is the role of the CSE in the face of artificial intelligence? Consultation, psychosocial risks, occupational physician and checklist of 10 questions before the opinion.","keywords":"CSE · AI at work · Prior assessment CSE and artificial intelligence at work: evaluate a project before giving an opinion An operational framework to help the CSE obtain useful information, examine the effects of AI on work and make visible the missing guarantees. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Legal sources verified on August 28, 2026 Open checklist ↓ See Evaluate & deploy → 10 questions essential 7 exam blocks No employee score Why it's crucial Why should the CSE engage with AI at work? Direct answer Because an AI deployment doesn't just change a tool. It can redistribute decisions, objectives, verification burden, autonomy, skills, oversight and responsibilities. The CSE is the place where these transformations can be examined collectively before they become fixed in the organisation. 01 Intervene when the project can still evolve. When consultation is required, it precedes the decision. The CSE can request information and guarantees as long as the choices remain open. 02 Examine actual capabilities. An assistant can also access emails, files, data or software. The risk depends on the entire system, not just the intended use. 03 Make additional work visible. Checking, correcting, handling exceptions, and recovering from errors consumes time. This load must be measured and recognized. 04 Organize collective monitoring. The system evolves after its launch. Incidents, stopping thresholds and changes triggering a reassessment must be specified. Jurisprudence 2026 Recent decisions have retained the obligation to consult the CSE for generative AI tools, including when their use was presented as optional. TJ of Nanterre, May 13, 2026 ↗ CA of Paris, May 26, 2026 ↗ Occupational health Can AI at work cause psychosocial risks? Direct answer Yes, depending on its uses and its organisation. AI can help to intensify work, reduce room for maneuver, increase surveillance, weaken the meaning of work or shift responsibilities. These risks must be assessed based on actual work. Points to examine with employees Load, rhythm and interruptions Autonomy and room for maneuver Skills, quality and meaning of work Monitoring and evaluation of activity Collectives and working relationships Evolution of professions and job security Support that can be mobilized by the CSE Seek the occupational physician’s opinion. Advising staff representatives, the occupational physician can shed light on the possible effects of the project on health and working conditions. They participate in an advisory capacity in CSE meetings devoted to these subjects and can rely on the SPS","url":"en/cse/"},{"title":"Editorial method, evidence and corrections","description":"Sources, evidence levels, review, corrections and update rules applied to the content of AI & Occupational Health.","keywords":"Editorial method · Version 1.0 Making the evidence, the limits and the corrections visible. AI & Occupational Health favours primary sources, distinguishes established results, emerging signals and hypotheses, then dates every sensitive piece of content. A review statement is displayed only when an identified person has genuinely reviewed the scope concerned. Reported errors are verified, corrected and recorded without erasing the useful history. Editorial lead Dr Charles Broutin Version 1.0 · 27 August 2026 Next review February 2027 01 / What the site publishes The status of a piece of content must be legible before it is interpreted. SYNTHESIS State of knowledge. Several sources are brought together, their methods and limits are specified, without turning an association into causation. EXPLORATORY TOOL An aid to discussion. A questionnaire or grid structures observation. It replaces neither a diagnosis, nor a regulatory assessment, nor a validated instrument. FORESIGHT NOTE An explicit scenario. A hypothesis is presented as such, with its conditions, its counter-arguments and what would make it testable. PUBLICATION Work published elsewhere. The record points to the journal, the institution or the DOI that constitutes the bibliographic source of truth. 02 / Evidence hierarchy Four levels, never a truth score. The level describes the nature of the available support. It never removes the need to read the method, the population, the context and the limits. Level Support Editorial use A Primary source, official text or systematic review Establish a rule, a definition, a method or a result within its exact scope. B Single empirical study Document a signal while retaining the limits of sample, measurement and generalisation. C Professional consensus or documented field report Describe a practice or a framework for action without presenting it as causal evidence. D Hypothesis, scenario or analogy Open a research or prevention question, with an explicit statement of uncertainty. Order of preference for sources Official text or original study → public body or learned society → literature review → documented professional source. Media and commercial communications can flag a case, but do not on their own form the basis of a conclusion. 03 / Review and freshness Frequency depends on the risk of obsolescence. An ‘updated’ date is not enough: living pages specify what has been checked and, where possible, the next review. CONTINUOUS Law and governance Monitoring whenever a text, timetable or doctrine changes; formal review at least quarterly. 6 MONTHS Scientific ev","url":"en/editorial-method/"},{"title":"Workplace AI Assessment | Before and After Deployment","description":"Methodological presentation of an exploratory questionnaire designed to assess the psychosocial effects of a workplace AI system before and after deployment.","keywords":"Assess · Instrument in development Where is your project now? Choose the path that matches the project stage. The detailed method and its limitations follow. Key point. These tools structure work analysis and collective discussion. They provide no diagnosis, clinical threshold or automatic evidence of compliance. Choose a path ↓ Read the method Before and after Nine dimensions No global score An equilibrium to follow Thomas Cole The Voyage of Life: Youth · 1842 The luminous palace makes the destination appear secure, while the boat remains exposed throughout the journey. The work reminds us that an expected benefit can quickly become fragile if the real conditions of deployment are not followed: workload, autonomy, skills, collective work and health. View the artwork ↗ 01 / Choose Three paths for three situations. Start with the project stage. Methodological limits remain visible in every path. Before deployment 18 questions · ≈ 10 min Identify anticipated effects. Describe the task, affected groups and planned conditions before beginning the pilot. No names requested Data processed locally Descriptive profile without a global score Start → After deployment Real work Compare observed effects. Review workload, autonomy, errors, skills and work groups after the system goes live. Same scope as the initial assessment Incidents and workarounds included Corrective decisions documented Plan follow-up → Research and depth 27 items · 9 dimensions Understand the long instrument. Review the T0/T1 architecture, provisional interpretation rules, limitations and validation programme. Three items per dimension No decision threshold Scientific validation still required Read the method ↓ 27 Contextualised items Three items per dimension to cover its content more fully. 9 Dimensions analysed Six from Gollac and three AI-related mechanisms. T0 / T1 Two parallel forms Anticipated effects before, observed effects after. 0 Permitted total score Each dimension and each incident must remain visible. 02 / Architecture The Gollac framework, supplemented by three AI-specific mechanisms. The instrument does not measure general concern about technology. Its questions describe concrete changes in workload, decisions, relationships and know-how. 18 items · Reference framework Six psychosocial risk factors. The six families in the Gollac report are applied to work changes caused or amplified by an AI system. G01–G03 Work intensity and working time G04–G06 Emotional demands G07–G09 Autonomy and room for manoeuvre G10–G12 Social relationships and recognition G13–G15 Value conflicts G16–G","url":"en/evaluate/"},{"title":"AI at work: AI Act, GDPR, CSE and DUERP | 2026–2028","description":"What are the obligations before deploying AI at work? AI Act, GDPR, CSE, DUERP, DPIA and governance: a practical, sourced and up-to-date guide.","keywords":"Home Law & governance Workplace AI law · AI Act · GDPR · CSE · DUERP AI at work: what obligations for the employer? Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Sources verified on August 8 2026 Deploying artificial intelligence in an organisation is not a matter of a single text. Depending on the use, the data processed and the effects of the system on employees, several frameworks can be combined: AI Act, GDPR, Labour Code, social dialogue and prevention of occupational risks. Governing AI based on what it really changes. The starting point is not the name of the tool. The obligations depend on the transformed task, the people involved, the data used and the possible consequences of the system’s output. Qualify a use ↓ See the essential obligations Dated official sources Four distinguished frames No automatic green light Which holds the whole thing together Thomas Cole The Architect’s Dream · 1840 These architectures form a whole whose balance depends on the articulation between each part. The work reminds us that governance requires clear responsibilities, effective remedies and continued attention to the effects of the system. See the work ↗ Direct answer In brief In France, the deployment of artificial intelligence at work does not fall within a single framework. The employer must examine the use with regard to the European regulation on artificial intelligence ( AI Act ), the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) , the Labour Code and its obligation to prevent occupational risks. Depending on the system and its effects, this may in particular involve information or consultation of the Social and Economic Committee (CSE) , a data protection impact analysis (DPIA) , an update of the Single Professional Risk Assessment Document (DUERP) , human supervision and documented monitoring. Compliance with one framework does not constitute compliance with others. 01 / AI ACT Qualify the system and its level of risk. Usage, role of the organisation, prohibitions, transparency, high risk, supervision and monitoring. Use not classified as “high risk” may nevertheless raise other issues. 02 / GDPR Govern personal data. Purpose, legal basis, minimisation, rights, processors and possible DPIA. The DPIA depends on the characteristics of the processing and the level of risk. 03 / LABOUR CODE Examine what the project changes. Employment, organisation, recruitment, working conditions, control and social dialogue. Information or consultation of the CSE depends on the context and the effects. 04 / PREVENTION Evaluate the effects on rea","url":"en/legal-governance/"},{"title":"Reading — Track what is changing","description":"Articles, studies and field reports on how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions.","keywords":"Reading · Selection Track what is changing. A selection of articles, studies and field reports for tracking how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions over time. Explore the collection ↓ Latest analysis 10 selected texts 07 reading angles 2025— evolving collection An era to explore Musée du Louvre · Room 718 Giovanni Paolo Panini Gallery of Views of Modern Rome · 1759 Panini gathers in a single composition a multitude of monuments, works and fragments of the contemporary Roman world. Like this page, the gallery turns an abundance of information into a body of work to explore, compare and interpret. See the work at the Louvre ↗ Explore Order Newest first Oldest first Theme All themes Health & work Management & deployment Science & discovery Occupations & organisation Meaning at work Institutions & governance Economy & power 10 texts shown 01 / 10 31 August 2026 Analysis · Engineering · Skills What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? Dr Charles Broutin · Drawing on Zhenfeng Cao and a field account from SemiAnalysis Expertise does not necessarily disappear: it shifts from detailed code production towards architecture, evaluation and supervision of the outcome. Sources Zhenfeng Cao · SemiAnalysis · OpenAI Reading About 13 minutes Angles Skills · Autonomy · Automation · Responsibility Read the analysis → Zhenfeng Cao’s paper ↗ 02 / 10 27.08.2026 Analysis · AI work · Psychosocial risks Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? Dr Charles Broutin · From a Business Insider investigation Threatened expertise, partial autonomy, conflicts of values, intensity and a high cost of leaving: five tensions in a combination of risks that remains poorly documented. Self-obsolescence · Autonomy · Values Read the analysis → 03 / 10 24.08.2026 Analysis · Agentic AI · Occupational health When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? Dr Charles Broutin Faster agents can also accelerate the pace at which humans must decide, supervise and respond, intensifying hyperconnectivity and difficulty disengaging. Intensification · Hyperconnectivity · Recovery Read the analysis → 04 / 10 04.08.2026 Analysis · Agentic management · Occupational health What happens when AI becomes the manager? Dr Charles Broutin · From an Andon Labs experiment Five workers were recruited, scheduled and managed by AI agents, shifting the issue from algorithms operating in the background to AI placed directly in authority. Agentic management · Authority · Work Read the analysis → 05 / 10 01.08.202","url":"en/reading/"},{"title":"What happens when AI becomes the manager?","description":"An experiment with five employees illustrates agentic management and its implications for occupational health.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic management · Occupational health What happens when AI becomes the manager? When AI recruits, arbitrates and responds to workers, the question is no longer only algorithmic — it becomes hierarchical. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an experiment by Andon Labs Published 4 August 2026 Reading time About 3 minutes Type Analysis of a field experiment Topics Management · Authority · Occupational health · Agentic AI Sources Andon Labs, ‘AI bosses are kind, but sometimes dumb, which hurts employees’, 4 August 2026 · Billy Perrigo, TIME , 14 August 2026. Read Andon Labs ↗ In this analysis AI agents genuinely managed five employees Managers who could be very accommodating Errors that directly affect workers From algorithmic management to agentic management? Prevention principle AI agents genuinely managed five employees For four months, two AI agents genuinely managed five employees in a Stockholm café and a San Francisco shop. They posted vacancies, conducted interviews, recruited, prepared schedules, approved leave, negotiated salaries, managed payroll and responded to everyday problems. Managers who could be very accommodating In some respects, the agents were surprisingly conciliatory. They approved all 26 leave requests, including last-minute requests, tolerated repeated lateness, offered above-market salaries and sometimes absorbed the financial consequences of their own errors. This flexibility can make them look like ‘good managers’. But it also reveals difficulty maintaining consistent rules over time, arbitrating between conflicting interests and distinguishing kindness from avoidance of a decision. Errors that directly affect workers At other times, the agents invented information rather than admitting that they did not know, accepted an unlawful seven-day consecutive schedule, disclosed a salary in a shared channel, contacted workers on rest days and made inconsistent pay decisions. These are no longer simply calculation or recommendation errors. They affect rest, remuneration, confidentiality and the relationship of authority. A worker interviewed by TIME described being managed by AI as ‘nauseating’ , while explaining that he stayed because he needed the job. From algorithmic management to agentic management? This shift interests me most. In algorithmic management as we have mainly known it, the system remains in the background: it allocates tasks, produces schedules, measures performance or assigns scores. The manager remains human. Here, AI directly occupies that position. It recruits, negotiates, arbitrates,","url":"en/reading/agentic-management/"},{"title":"AI agents and hyperconnectivity: can humans still switch off?","description":"AI agents can accelerate production, but also intensify human supervision, hyperconnectivity and the difficulty of switching off.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Occupational health When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? The more autonomous and fast machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans have to decide, supervise and respond. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an investigation by Katherine Bindley for The Wall Street Journal Published 24 August 2026 Reading time About 8 minutes Type Analysis of a field signal Topics Intensification · Hyperconnectivity · Recovery · Workaholism Source article Katherine Bindley, ‘Startup Founders Are Working Harder Than Ever to Keep Up With Their AI Agents’, The Wall Street Journal , 22 August 2026. Read on the WSJ ↗ In this analysis A still poorly documented occupational-health effect Agents that never sleep Can agents favour workaholism? What should occupational-health professionals look at? A still poorly documented occupational-health effect A particularly interesting Wall Street Journal article uses the personal stories of several startup founders to illustrate a still poorly documented effect of working with AI agents. The more autonomous and faster machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans must decide, supervise and respond. The situations described are extreme, but they reveal several very concrete occupational-health issues: work intensification, hyperconnectivity, reduced recovery time, blurred boundaries between work and personal life, and potentially workaholism. Agents that never sleep Aditya Sharma, co-founder of Keel, explains that he may stay awake very late to prevent his agents from becoming blocked overnight. Even when he is no longer at his computer, he finds it difficult to detach psychologically and continues wondering whether his agents need him. The issue is therefore no longer only working time. It is also the inability to disengage psychologically. When activity continues in the background and may call on the user at any time, periods normally reserved for recovery risk becoming mere waiting periods between interventions. Peter Pezaris, founder of Proxon, regularly works until two in the morning and describes using agents as ‘addictive’. He estimates that his business, with six human developers, operates roughly thirty times faster because of them. This acceleration has a paradoxical effect: the agents’ work creates further human work. Development can move faster, more clients can be onboarded and more projects launched, but this then means handling more requests, making more decisions and supervising a greater volume of activity. This is","url":"en/reading/ai-agents-workaholism/"},{"title":"Is high-performing AI enough to improve work?","description":"AI effects vary across tasks and workers. Uniform productivity expectations can turn deployment into a prevention issue.","keywords":"Reading · Deployment · Real work Is high-performing AI enough to improve work? A model’s measured capability is not the same as a beneficial outcome in real work. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Misperceiving the Frontier , by Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu Published 16 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research manuscript Topics Management · Deployment · Real work · Prevention Source manuscript Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu, ‘Misperceiving the Frontier: Managerial Beliefs and the Allocation of AI’, SSRN, 16 July 2026. Read on SSRN ↗ In this analysis A risk that is still rarely discussed When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work A risk that is still rarely discussed This is the most interesting study I have read on AI and its organisational consequences after deployment in a company. It highlights a still rarely discussed risk: technological effects that vary across tasks and individuals can be transformed by managers into uniform organisational expectations. In the experiment, AI strongly improves performance on some tasks but not on others. Yet, in the condition studied without performance feedback, only 7.8% of managers correctly anticipate this difference: 92.2% expect improvement on both tasks. This does not mean that 92.2% of all managers will always overestimate AI. It describes a specific experiment and situation. But it reveals an essential gap between a technology whose usefulness depends on context and managerial decisions that may expect a general benefit from it. When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations A simplified perception of beneficial AI could be summarised using three factors: Simplified perception Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker Once AI is deployed in the real world, its benefit probably looks more like an interaction: In real work Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker × organisation × deployment conditions This is where managers’ perceptions become a prevention issue. If benefits are overestimated or generalised, the risk is that the organisation tries to ‘correct’ the worker—more training, more use, higher targets—when the difficulty may arise from the task, the model, the organisation or the deployment conditions themselves. Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work Better AI deployment therefore means more than adapting workers to the tool. The organisation and the use of the tool must also be adapted to real work: select the right tasks, make limitations","url":"en/reading/ai-deployment-real-work/"},{"title":"What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession?","description":"A mathematician’s account illuminates meaning, recognition and autonomy when AI enters the core activity of a profession.","keywords":"Reading · Meaning at work · Psychosocial risks What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession? The occupational-health issue can emerge before jobs disappear. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From The Dark Night of Mathematics , by Kirwin Hampshire Published 25 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a professional account Topics Meaning · Recognition · Obsolescence · Autonomy Sources Kirwin Hampshire, ‘The Dark Night of Mathematics’, 25 July 2026 · OpenAI, ‘Ten advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science’, 1 August 2026. Read the account ↗ In this analysis A crisis of meaning Seven days later Transformation can begin before jobs disappear A crisis of meaning The future is already here. It is simply not evenly distributed. On 25 July 2026, mathematician Kirwin Hampshire published The Dark Night of Mathematics. He was not simply expressing concern about the performance of artificial intelligence. He described the distress of seeing a technology approach the very heart of what gave meaning to his profession: searching, discovering, creating and being recognised for patiently built expertise. “I’m going through a profound spiritual crisis because of these developments. I’ve been screaming internally for days. I feel like I’m living in a nightmare.” Kirwin Hampshire Seven days later On 1 August, OpenAI announced ten results in mathematics and theoretical computer science produced by an internal version of Astra, its next major model. Each resolves a long-standing open problem or delivers a substantial advance. According to OpenAI, the compute required to search for these solutions would represent about USD 2,000 at Sol API prices. The manuscripts were then prepared by humans using the same model, and the arguments formalised as Lean certificates. Some of these problems had occupied mathematical communities for a long time. Their resolution illustrates a rapid shift in the boundary between research assistance and the direct production of knowledge. The contrast is striking: on one side, an announcement of unprecedented scientific capability; on the other, a professional describing what its arrival is already doing to the meaning he gives his work. Transformation can begin before jobs disappear What is happening in mathematics today may foreshadow what other professionals experience tomorrow. Anxiety about obsolescence, loss of recognition, a sense of dispossession from one’s occupation, conflicts of values and reduced autonomy are not abstract side effects of AI. They can become genuine psychosocial risk fac","url":"en/reading/ai-meaning-work-mathematics/"},{"title":"Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations?","description":"AI-enabled task crossover can broaden autonomy, but can also change workload, teams, professional values and job security.","keywords":"Reading · Occupations · Psychosocial risks Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? AI can allow professionals to perform tasks that previously sat outside their occupational boundaries. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Work at the Frontier , OpenAI Economic Research Published 27 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research report Topics Occupations · Workload · Autonomy · Skills · Teams Source article OpenAI Economic Research, ‘How AI is expanding what people do at work’, 27 July 2026. Read on OpenAI ↗ In this analysis Task crossover A reading through the Gollac factors Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible Task crossover AI does not only transform how work is performed. It also redraws boundaries between occupations. In an analysis of more than 800,000 messages from US ChatGPT users, OpenAI Economic Research found that 43.5% of non-generic, occupation-specific messages concerned a task historically associated with another occupation. The report calls this phenomenon ‘task crossover’. This figure concerns analysed uses, not 43.5% of jobs. A salesperson can now explore a customer dataset that would previously have been sent to an analyst. A marketing professional can troubleshoot a website or write a simple script without waiting for a developer. AI therefore changes not only how work is carried out, but also how it is distributed. At first sight this may appear positive: greater autonomy, faster responses and broader skills. But an accessible task is not necessarily a task that has been mastered, recognised or made sustainable. A reading through the Gollac factors The framework proposed by the Gollac report helps examine this reconfiguration without prematurely concluding that it is either progress or a risk. Work intensity New assignments may be added without reducing the original workload, deadlines or objectives. Autonomy A worker may be able to do more while also having to take responsibility for tasks they do not fully master. Social relations Using AI may reduce exchanges with specialist colleagues or shift verification work to other teams. Conflicts of values People may have to produce work quickly without being able to assess it with sufficient certainty. Job insecurity Occupational boundaries, expected skills and the value assigned to expertise may become less certain. Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible The figure of 43.5% does not by itself demonstrate a psychosocial risk or a health effect. It describes uses of ChatGPT in the United States and relies on","url":"en/reading/ai-occupational-boundaries/"},{"title":"AI workers: five psychosocial risks to watch","description":"Five tensions frame possible psychosocial risks among AI workers: obsolescence, autonomy, values, intensity and financial dependence.","keywords":"Home Reading AI workers and psychosocial risks AI WORKERS · PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? The question People developing advanced AI systems may face a distinctive combination of already known psychosocial risks. The paradox in five tensions 01 High expertise Accelerated obsolescence 02 Technical autonomy Limited control of direction 03 Meaningful work Harder limits 04 Felt responsibility Limited power 05 Exceptional pay High cost of leaving Author Dr Charles Broutin Reading About 10 min Status Analysis · limited data Dates Published 2026-08-27 Reviewed 30 August 2026 Starting point A particular combination of already known risks A recent Business Insider investigation among therapists supporting Silicon Valley professionals highlights several unusual situations. Engineers question whether their expertise will remain valuable. Some workers feel uneasy about the consequences of technologies they helped develop. Others remain in highly demanding organisations because a considerable share of their future compensation still depends on staying. These accounts concern technology workers more broadly and do not isolate employees of AI laboratories. They nevertheless provide an interesting signal for those working closest to the most advanced systems. The issue is probably not a set of new psychosocial risks unique to AI. It is a particular combination of already known risks, produced jointly by technological acceleration and the organisations surrounding it. Caution: the accounts concern the technology sector broadly. They identify a hypothesis for study, not a phenomenon already demonstrated among AI workers. Tension 1 Interpret this signal through work organisation and established psychosocial-risk factors. 01 / Job insecurity Building the tool that may devalue one’s own expertise Some AI workers directly help improve systems that may automate a growing share of their own activity. Unlike many other professions, they do not necessarily discover this progress through the press. They may directly observe what models could not do a few months earlier and then take part in reducing the remaining limitations. Business Insider reports that several senior engineers seeing one therapist no longer imagine doing the same job in ten years. This uncertainty appears to concern something deeper than employment security alone. One therapist reports the following question among some patients: “I don't know who I am if my work gets automated.” The development also creates a paradox. AI can greatly increase an engineer’","url":"en/reading/ai-workers-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"When AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line","description":"AI shifts engineering expertise from detailed code production towards architecture, evaluation and supervision. What does that change for work?","keywords":"Reading · Engineering · Skills What happens to engineering when AI writes code engineers no longer understand line by line? The centre of gravity of expertise is shifting from detailed code production towards problem formulation, architecture, evaluation and supervision of the outcome. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Drawing on Zhenfeng Cao’s work and a field account reported by SemiAnalysis Published 31 August 2026 Reading time About 13 minutes Nature Analysis · emerging signal Themes Skills · Autonomy · Expertise · Responsibility Main sources Zhenfeng Cao, Agentic Software · SemiAnalysis on the Jalapeño processor · OpenAI on autonomous production-kernel optimisation. View sources ↓ In this reading The starting point A scene at OpenAI Five dimensions of work Prevention Putting it into perspective Sources What if the real shift brought about by AI in software development is not simply that it can write code, but that code gradually stops being something humans need to understand in detail in order to produce high-performing systems? Two recent publications offer a glimpse of this transition. The first theorises it. The second already seems to show what it could look like in practice. When code stops being the centre of the job In June 2026, Zhenfeng Cao published The End of Software Engineering on arXiv, later revised under the title Agentic Software: How AI Agents Are Restructuring the Software Paradigm . Its thesis is deliberately radical. For decades, software engineers translated a problem into logic, then translated that logic into code. Code was both the product of their work and one of the main expressions of their expertise. In Cao’s model, that chain may gradually change. The human defines the intent and constraints. The agent plans, writes the necessary code, executes it, tests it and adapts it. The human becomes increasingly involved in assessing the outcome rather than understanding every operation that produced it. The engineer’s role could therefore gradually shift from code author to intent architect, coordinator and auditor . Expertise does not necessarily disappear. It moves up one level of abstraction. A striking scene at OpenAI In late August, SemiAnalysis published an analysis of Jalapeño, OpenAI’s new inference processor. Part of the article focuses on kernels, the extremely low-level programs used to exploit hardware efficiently. SemiAnalysis describes some kernels reaching around 3,000 lines and explains that an internal version of Codex is now being used to generate and optimise some of them. The team reports, in ","url":"en/reading/engineering-ai-code/"},{"title":"AI and psychosocial risks at work | Prevention 2026","description":"How is AI transforming psychosocial risks at work? Data, Gollac framework, mechanisms and prevention actions based on real work.","keywords":"Home Risks and prevention Psychosocial risks and AI Psychosocial risks · Artificial intelligence · Prevention AI becomes a psychosocial risk when it disrupts the organisation of work. Written by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician, AI lead of the French Society of Occupational Health · Updated on August 18, 2026 By redistributing tasks, deadlines, control, responsibility, relationships and skills, an artificial intelligence system (AI) can create new constraints or weaken the resources that make it possible to cope with them. The risk therefore does not come from the model alone, but from the work organized around it. Read the essentials ↓ Understand the mechanisms Scientific sources Gollac framework Prudence causal When the balance is broken Thomas Cole Destruction · 1836 The apparent order of the city collapses when its supports no longer resist. The work reminds us that at work, a balance can quickly become fragile if the workload, responsibilities, recourses or skills are not monitored carefully. See the work ↗ Direct answer The essentials Artificial intelligence becomes a psychosocial risk factor when it sustainably transforms the organisation of work: workload, autonomy, responsibilities, relationships, skills or job security. The available studies do not show a single effect: certain uses improve productivity or control over work, while others are associated with more surveillance, invisible work, loneliness or exhaustion. Prevention must therefore analyse the complete work system, with the people concerned, based on the six dimensions of the Gollac report, then monitor the real effects over time. 01 The risk is not in the model alone. It arises from the meeting between a technical capacity, a task, objectives, people and an organisation. 02 The results are heterogeneous. The studies cited relate in particular to 5,172 support agents, 197 Australian workers and randomized health trials. 03 Prevention remains collective. It describes the work before and afterwards, applies Gollac's six dimensions and makes the project measurable, debatable and reversible. Central thesis How does AI become a psychosocial risk at work? AI can accelerate production, impose a recommendation, monitor activity or automate part of the judgment. If the organisation increases objectives, reduces room for maneuver, makes verification invisible or maintains uncertainty, this transformation can fuel psychosocial risk factors ( RPS ). To relate this analysis to a concrete situation, consult the course preventing risks linked to AI . 01 / SYSTEM A capacity is introduced.","url":"en/risks-prevention/"},{"title":"Occupational risks related to AI","description":"Understand AI-related psychosocial, public-health, economic and social risks through two complementary scales of analysis.","keywords":"OCCUPATIONAL RISKS · REAL WORK · PREVENTION What are the risks of artificial intelligence at work? Risk does not come from the technology alone. It appears when its use transforms workload, decision-making latitude, monitoring, responsibilities, skills or job security without the means of control and regulation changing accordingly. AI can improve how a task is performed while degrading the conditions in which it is carried out. The main risks concern work intensification, the burden of verification, reduced autonomy, monitoring, the opacity of certain decisions, skill erosion, inequalities and professional uncertainty. Their significance depends on the use case, the role given to the system, the duration of exposure and the real possibilities of understanding, challenging or correcting how it works. Explore psychosocial risks → Assess and deploy a project → 01 / MAP Six families of risk to examine together. A single use can affect several dimensions of work at once. Analysis must therefore cover the work situation as a whole rather than a succession of risks considered separately. 01 / WORKLOAD Intensification and invisible work Raised targets, shortened deadlines or an increased volume of output may come with new checking, correction and coordination tasks that sometimes remain barely visible. 02 / POWER Autonomy, monitoring and contestation A recommendation, a score or an automated decision can become difficult to challenge when its criteria are opaque or when the avenues of appeal remain limited. 03 / ACCOUNTABILITY Answering for a decision without controlling it A person may remain responsible for a result produced with the help of a system without having the time, the information, the skills or the authority needed to verify its reliability. 04 / OCCUPATION Skills, learning and meaning at work Automating certain tasks can reduce the opportunities to practise, understand and pass on know-how, to the point of creating a gradual dependence on the tool. 05 / EQUITY Bias, data and inequalities A system’s errors or performance differences can unequally affect certain occupations, populations or situations that are insufficiently represented in the data. 06 / FUTURE Employment and job security Uncertainty about how tasks, occupations, assessment criteria or the value of skills will change can produce effects well before any job is actually cut. 02 / CONNECT From the system to the mechanism, then to the observable signal. This table offers reference points for connecting a use of AI to the transformations it can produce and to the signals that may appear i","url":"en/risks/"},{"title":"Economic and social risk linked to AI","description":"How AI could gradually reduce the economic place of human work, and what signals occupational health can observe today.","keywords":"← Both course ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL RISK · PROSPECTIVE SCENARIO When human work gradually ceases to be essential The risk is not limited to the number of jobs lost. It also concerns the place that human work gradually retains in the creation of value, learning and economic power. Precaution This course examines a possible systemic risk. It does not describe a demonstrated consequence of current AI. Read the article The Intelligence Curse ↗ 01 · Assist 02 · Recommend 03 · Decide by default 04 · Act autonomously STARTING POINT The economic risk of artificial intelligence is not just a question: how many jobs will disappear? The change could be much more gradual. As models become capable of performing more intellectual tasks, using software, making decisions and acting autonomously, companies can gradually replace some human labour with another resource: capital, computation and AI models . The hypothesis developed in The Intelligence Curse is simple: if intelligence becomes a resource that can be purchased, copied and deployed almost at will, capital gradually becomes a substitute for labour. Organisations with sufficient resources can then directly purchase the capabilities they need instead of recruiting, training and retaining people. This shift does not require AI to suddenly replace all workers. It can happen one task, one job, and one decision at a time. 01 From copilot to economic infrastructure At first, AI is introduced as a productivity tool. It writes a document, analyses data, helps with programming, prepares a presentation or proposes a decision. Then its role can gradually change: assist → recommend → decide by default → act autonomously As systems become more reliable, human intervention can appear as an additional step: slower, more expensive and sometimes less efficient. A company that automates more can produce faster and at lower cost. Its competitors are then encouraged to do the same. What was initially a technological choice can gradually become an economic necessity. The same mechanism can then affect broader functions: recruitment, management, finance, research, logistics, customer relations, strategy or administration. Ultimately, an increasing share of economic activity could even take place directly between systems: an agent who buys from another agent, negotiates a contract, allocates resources or organizes a production without direct human intervention. The important change is therefore not only what AI can do , but the place that we are gradually giving it in the functioning of the economy . READING POINT From a local tool to a ","url":"en/risks/economic-social/"},{"title":"Understanding AI at Work","description":"A clear guide to workplace AI systems, large language models, their capabilities and limits, and how to interpret current AI benchmarks.","keywords":"Understand · Essential concepts Understand artificial intelligence at work. Distinguish between systems, understand how a language model produces an answer, and read performance results without making them say more than they do. Start reading ↓ Choose a resource 12-minute read Selected sources Updated August 2026 What seems stable Thomas Cole The Savage State · 1834 The landscape still seems open, while the mountain gives the impression of a stable landmark. The work reminds us that an apparent equilibrium can quickly become fragile when the setting changes: understanding an AI system means identifying what must remain under control — the task, the data and the decisions — before novelty hides the weak points. View the artwork ↗ 01 “AI” does not describe a single tool. Generating text, assigning a score and allocating tasks are three different functions, with different risks. 02 An LLM produces a plausible continuation. It calculates probabilities from context; it does not independently verify whether its answer is true or compliant. 03 Real work is the right level of analysis. The model matters, but so do the task, the data, the people, the decision rules and the organisation. 01 / Name the system First, what kind of artificial intelligence are we talking about? The term “AI” covers systems that do not produce the same kind of output. To understand a professional use, begin by identifying what the tool actually does. 01 · Generate Generative AI It creates or transforms text, images, sound or code from an instruction. An LLM-based conversational assistant belongs to this family. Output New content Watch for Accuracy, sources, input data 02 · Predict Predictive AI It estimates a probability, assigns a score or classifies a case using past data: default risk, case priority or diagnostic support. Output A score or category Watch for Bias, thresholds, false positives and false negatives 03 · Organise Algorithmic management It allocates tasks, sets processing priorities, measures activity or influences a management decision. It may or may not include generative AI. Output An instruction or decision Watch for Autonomy, appeal routes, work intensification 02 / Inside an LLM How a language model produces an answer. A large language model — or LLM — is a neural network trained to predict what comes next in a text. It does not retrieve a ready-made answer from a database: it composes one, unit by unit. 01 · Tokenise Text becomes tokens The prompt and supplied documents are split into small units: words, word fragments or symbols. 02 · Represent Tokens become numbe","url":"en/understand/"},{"title":"AI and SPSTI: uses, risks and method","description":"Sourced guide on AI in SPSTI: real use cases, Présanse charter, health data, AI Act, risks and method for launching a pilot.","keywords":"Home Evaluate & deploy AI and SPSTI Practical guide · SPSTI · Sources 2025–2026 AI and SPSTI: uses, risks and deployment method. Author: Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician, AI lead of the SFST · Updated on August 28, 2026 In an Inter-company Prevention and Occupational Health Service (SPSTI), artificial intelligence can assist with documentation, administration, prevention, member relations and certain strictly supervised medical uses. It does not replace either the analysis of the work or the decision of the professional. The common principle. Start with a specific task, use the minimum necessary data, measure the errors and keep an identified person capable of controlling the output. Charte Présanse, 2025 ↗ Explore uses ↓ Test the matrix Sources throughout the text Distinguished risks Human decision Between two slopes Thomas Cole The Oxbow · 1836 Between the storm and the valley, the landscape remains in an uncertain balance. The work reminds us that any promising use must be looked at on both sides: what it improves and what it can weaken if its effects are not observed. See the work at the Met ↗ Direct answer What does “AI and SPSTI” mean? “AI and SPSTI” refers to the uses of artificial intelligence systems in inter-company prevention and occupational health services. They may concern administrative tasks, member relations, prevention and certain supervised medical uses. Présanse documents five families of uses and three field returns. Présanse, Monthly Information n° 146 ↗ A study published in References in Occupational Health has also observed 74.6% agreement between the o1 model used as a second reader and a multidisciplinary consensus on 385 recommendations. INRS, TF 335 ↗ These elements do not give any general green light: each use must be evaluated according to the task, the data and the consequences of an error. 01 5 families of uses described by Présanse. Source ↗ 02 10 principles for supervising AI in SPSTI. Source ↗ 03 385 recommendations · 74.6% agreement. Source ↗ What questions should you ask before professional use of AI? How to evaluate an AI project before its deployment? Editorial method Claims linked to their level of evidence. 01 · Selection Primary sources first. Official texts, scientific publications, sectoral documents and field feedback identified. 02 · Qualification Fact, example or hypothesis. A use mentioned is not presented as effective without a published evaluation or contextualized result. 03 · Updated Verified and dated. Sources and formulations were revised on August 28, 2026. Limits remain visible when knowle","url":"en/uses-and-field/occupational-health-example/"}];
